  "href": "/life/2026/02/04/prado",
  "title": "Madrid Vibes & Masterpieces at the Prado 🇪🇸🎨",
  "date": "2026-02-03T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Madrid Vibes & Masterpieces at the Prado 🇪🇸🎨\n\nFinally arrived in Madrid! 📍 After a bit of a rainy start that left all my clothes soaked, I had to get creative. My hostel offers laundry for €9, which is a lifesaver, but while everything was in the wash, I had to go out for breakfast wearing the only dry things I had left: a swimsuit and a winter jacket. 👙🧥 Quite the fashion statement for the Spanish winter!\n\nMe\n\nFirst Impressions: The City 🏙️\n\nWalking around the city center, Madrid feels surprisingly brand new. Despite being the capital for over 400 years, it’s all wide roads and grand, polished buildings rather than ancient ruins.\n\nPalacio Real de Madrid\n\nPalacio Real de Madrid: They say it’s one of the largest palaces in Europe, but honestly? It felt a bit compact! The gardens are beautiful though, and completely free to wander. 🌳\n\nParque de el Retiro\n\nParque de el Retiro: This is the heart of the city. There’s a massive square lake, street musicians filling the air with tunes, and tons of locals jogging. It’s the perfect spot to decompress. 🎶🏃‍♂️\n\nThe Main Event: Museo del Prado 🖼️✨\n\nThe Prado is conveniently located right near the park. Pro tip: tickets are free after 18:00, but the line was absolutely massive, so I opted to pay the €15 the next day to actually have time to see it.\n\nHere is the lowdown on the Prado experience:\n\nMuseo del Prado\n\nIt’s Huge: I spent 4 hours wandering through the galleries and still didn’t see everything. The scale of the collection is mind-blowing! 🤯\n\nThe Tech Struggle: Warning! There is almost zero data signal inside. I wanted to use AI as my personal tour guide, but the signal just wouldn't cooperate. Luckily, the special exhibition area has free Wi-Fi! 📶\n\nNo Photos Allowed: This was a bit frustrating. The Prado is very strict about cameras—even if you're just trying to snap a photo of the name tag to know the artist. 🚫📸 I believe museums should be about spreading knowledge, but they are very old-school here. I think museum should provide multiple options for different people. For example, I am more interested in the materials on the artwork than the story of it. The modern AI can do that.\n\nMuse Muse\n\nThe Best Hack: Since my AI plan failed, I downloaded MuseMuse. It’s a free walking tour app and the experience was a solid 5 stars! ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Highly recommend it for navigating the maze of masterpieces.\n\nA Note on Spanish Food & Travel 🥘🚆\n\nfood\n\nThe Diet: Spanish food is tasty, but I’m desperately craving some green! I wish they’d throw a few more onions or tomatoes into the mix. 🥗\n\nstation\n\nTransport Lessons: I headed to Atocha station only to find my train was cancelled due to recent railway maintenance. I booked through Trip.com, and while they emailed me, the messages were all in Spanish and I thought they were just ads! 📩❌\n\nPro Tip: Always book through the official railway website. Third-party apps like Omio or Trip.com are a nightmare when it comes to getting refunds for cancellations. Luckily, I stayed calm and booked a bus instead. This isn't my first rodeo with cancelled trains on this trip! 🚌💨",
